601 0 obj
<<
/Title (The naturally occurring substance kojic acid, which is produced by several fungi including Aspergillus oryzae, has recently gained a lot of interest in the beauty, dermatological, and food preservation industries. The mycelium of Aspergillus oryzae was grown on steamed rice when Saito found KA in 1907. The "koji" in the name of the fungus that produced it is where the term "kojic acid" first appeared. Yabuta gave the chemical the name "kojic acid" in 1913. The leftover residue from fermenting rice for malting is where the Japanese word "koji," meaning steamed rice, comes from. Yabuta also gave a detailed breakdown of kojic acid's chemical  composition.  There after, additional studies)
/Dest [10 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 599 0 R
/Next 602 0 R
>>
endobj
602 0 obj
<<
/Title (have been carried out to investigate its various potential uses. It was later shown that fungus create the metabolite 5-hydroxy-12-hydromethylpyrone. The Japanese believe that kojic acid has health-promoting characteristics, hence they include it in their diet. A wide variety of carbohydrates can be converted into kojic acid by a wide variety of anaerobic bacteria. About 1 gram of kojic acid per kilogram of body weight is the lethal dose for two living things. There are several industries that can benefit economically from kojic acid. The medicinal community makes use of kojic acid for its fungicidal and antibacterial properties. For a long time, it has been used as an additive inthe food industry to delay the)
/Dest [10 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 599 0 R
